\use{document}

\title {Math Alphabets, Phantoms and Smash}
\author {The texish project}
\date {June 2026}
\maketitle

\section {The math alphabets}

A formula draws its variables from the Mathematical Alphanumeric blocks of the
math font. Each alphabet command remaps the letters of its argument into one of
those blocks, so the same word can be set in any of the standard math typefaces:

$$ \mathrm{Roman} \quad \mathbf{Bold} \quad \mathit{Italic} \quad \mathsf{Sans} \quad \mathtt{Typewriter} $$

The two decorative alphabets that mathematicians reach for constantly are
blackboard bold, for the number systems, and calligraphic and fraktur, for
structures and ideals:

$$ \mathbb{N} \subset \mathbb{Z} \subset \mathbb{Q} \subset \mathbb{R} \subset \mathbb{C} $$

$$ \mathcal{F} : \mathfrak{A} \to \mathfrak{B}, \qquad \mathfrak{p} \subset \mathcal{O}_K $$

A worked sentence mixes several at once: let $\mathbb{F}$ be a field, $V$ a
vector space over $\mathbb{F}$, and $\mathcal{L}(V)$ the algebra of operators on
it; write $\mathbf{x}$ for a column vector and $\mathrm{tr}\, A$ for a trace.

\section {Phantoms}

A \emph{phantom} reserves the size of its argument without printing it. The
plain \texttt{phantom} reserves both width and height, so it can push text
sideways by exactly the width of some other word. Compare the gap left by
$\mathbf{a}\phantom{bbbb}\mathbf{c}$ --- the space between is the width of four
b's, drawn as nothing.

The \texttt{vphantom} form reserves only height, the usual way to give a row of
fractions a common strut so they share a baseline:

$$ \frac{1}{2} \qquad \frac{a}{b} \qquad \frac{x^2}{y_0} $$

\section {Smash}

Where a phantom prints nothing but keeps its size, \texttt{smash} does the
opposite: it prints its argument but reports zero height and depth, so a tall
construction stops pushing its line apart. The smashed radical $\smash{\sqrt{2}}$
sits on the same line spacing as the ordinary text around it, while an unsmashed
$\sqrt{2}$ opens the line up to clear the surd.

